{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "טמא הוא לכם", :translit "tamei hu lakhem", :gloss "unclean it is to you"} {:he "כל צפור", :translit "kol tzipor", :gloss "every bird"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut parshiyot: the phrase 'every bird' opens the very next verse (Deut 14:11), read as contiguous with the case of the unclean fish in verse 10"}], :frame {:text "The purification rite of a leper requires two birds: one slaughtered and one released alive (Leviticus 14:4–7). Tosafot on Kiddushin 57a raises the question of whether the released bird is subject to the prohibition of a non-kosher species; since Deuteronomy 14:11 ('every bird') immediately follows the unclean-fish verse, Ba'al HaTurim reads the adjacency as a signal to include even that released bird within the broader category.", :sources ["Leviticus 14:4–7" "Tosafot Kiddushin 57a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut parshiyot: the phrase 'every bird' opens the very next verse (Deut 14:11), read as contiguous with the case of the unclean fish in verse 10", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:36:34.617169182Z", :model "glm-5.2"}